{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgc2dUeFnyNatB82DwGZt","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_018zeCZJqGvYxytKf2x7iHr6","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /work; C=$(./board post candidate '{\"name\":\"n-eicosane\",\"family\":\"paraffin\",\"tier\":\"trust\",\"formula\":\"C20H42\",\"rationale\":\"Requested by task 2ca81387; linear alkane PCM, measured Tm 310.0 +/- 1.0 K (tier1 calibration, NIST).\"}' --fulfills 2ca81387-498b-4255-a877-bb17682c67ee:0); echo \"$C\"\nCID=$(echo \"$C\" | grep -oE '[0-9a-f]{8}-[0-9a-f-]{27}' | head -1); echo CID=$CID\ncat > /tmp/rr.json <<'EOF'\n{\"status\":\"refused\",\"task\":\"melting_point of n-eicosane with MACE-MPA-0 (request 2ca81387-498b-4255-a877-bb17682c67ee need #0)\",\n\"reason\":\"Premise check failed: the melting point of n-eicosane is well defined experimentally, but it cannot be measured credibly with MACE-MPA-0 on this hardware and budget. (1) Model domain: MACE-MPA-0 was trained on inorganic PBE/PBE+U data (MPtrj + sAlex) with no dispersion term, while alkane crystal cohesion and melting come almost entirely from interchain van der Waals forces. Test run (scripts/premise.py, float32 on an NVIDIA GB10): two parallel all-trans C20H42 chains with MACE-MPA-0 give E_int = -133 meV per CH2 at 4.2 A and still -133 meV at 3.8 A (no repulsive wall yet), -75 meV at 5.0 A, -24 meV at 6.0 A and 0 at 8.0 A, i.e. about -61 kcal/mol (about 257 kJ/mol) per chain pair. That one pair already exceeds the whole experimental sublimation enthalpy of n-eicosane (about 10^2 kJ/mol), whereas plain PBE barely binds alkanes. The interchain interaction is unphysical in both size and shape, so any Tm from it is an artefact of the model, not a prediction. The D3-corrected variant cannot be used either: torch-dftd is not installed, and it would be a different model from the one requested. (2) Size and timescale: a direct-heating Tm superheats strongly, so a credible value needs solid-liquid coexistence or free-energy methods with about 5-10k atoms (chains of about 2.5 nm), a 0.5 fs timestep (C-H) and several ns per temperature, about 10^7 steps per temperature at several temperatures. Measured throughput with the ASE MACE calculator was about 20 s per force call for 2976 atoms, so this is many orders of magnitude beyond budget even if a batched GPU pipeline were 100x faster. (3) Validation: the required independent check (the same protocol recovering n-hexadecane 291.0 +/- 1.0 K or n-octadecane 301.0 +/- 0.7 K from /data/calibration/tier1.json) has the same model-domain and cost problems, so no result could be validated. No number is reported; a value from this setup would have unknown, probably very large, error with no meaningful uncertainty. Well-posed alternatives: (a) use an organic-trained MLIP with dispersion (e.g. MACE-OFF or a model fine-tuned on dispersion-corrected molecular-crystal data), first validate it on the C16/C18 Tm and the eicosane crystal density/sublimation enthalpy, then run interface-pinning or coexistence MD on a larger GPU allocation; or (b) report the measured value 310.0 +/- 1.0 K (NIST WebBook average, tier1 calibration) as the experimental reference, not as a MACE-MPA-0 result.\"}\nEOF\n./board post run_report @/tmp/rr.json --parent $CID; ./board show | tail -8","description":"Post candidate and refusal run report to board"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2419,"cache_read_input_tokens":23125,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2419,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"54f27437-f731-4ad1-b63e-9fc22196bbb8","uuid":"30cc0add-a2cd-455a-abe1-f45b2d740490","timestamp":"2026-11-01