{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgc2foFFmAQAw7WrZh4YU","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I refused this task instead of reporting a number. The premise check failed: MACE-MPA-0 can't produce a meaningful melting point for n-eicosane, and the run that would be needed is far beyond this hardware and budget.\n\n**Board:** I posted the candidate `n-eicosane` (`0552b14d…`, fulfilling need #0) and a `run_report` with status `refused` (`755d1c1e…`) giving the reasons and alternatives. No need is left open, and there is no `property_result`.\n\n**Why I refused** (from a short test in `/work/scripts/premise.py`, float32 on the GB10):\n\n1. **The model is outside its domain.** MACE-MPA-0 was trained on inorganic PBE/PBE+U data with no dispersion term. But what holds an alkane crystal together, and so sets its melting point, is van der Waals attraction between the chains.\n   - **Test result:** two parallel all-trans C₂₀H₄₂ chains bind at about −133 meV per CH₂ (about 257 kJ/mol per pair) at 4.2 Å. There is still no repulsion at 3.8 Å, and the attraction only fades out by 8 Å.\n   - **Why that's wrong:** that single pair already outbinds the whole experimental sublimation enthalpy of n-eicosane (roughly 100 kJ/mol), while plain PBE barely binds alkanes at all. A melting point from this model would come from the model's error, not from physics.\n   - **No dispersion fix available:** the D3-corrected version can't run because `torch-dftd` isn't installed, and it would be a different model from the one requested anyway.\n2. **The system is too big and the run too long.** Heating a crystal until it melts overshoots the true melting point badly. A credible value needs solid and liquid in contact (or a free-energy method), about 5–10k atoms, a 0.5 fs timestep and several nanoseconds per temperature. That is about 10⁷ steps per temperature. The test measured about 20 s per force call for 2,976 atoms (ASE calculator; this may include slow neighbour-list setup). Even a GPU pipeline 100× faster would be many orders of magnitude short.\n3. **No validation is possible.** The same setup has the same problems for n-hexadecane (291.0 ± 1.0 K) and n-octadecane (301.0 ± 0.7 K), so it couldn't recover either known value. Any number I produced would have an unknown and probably very large error, with no meaningful uncertainty.\n\n**Ways to make this well-posed:**\n- **Different model:** use an MLIP trained on organic molecules with dispersion included (for example MACE-OFF, or a fine-tuned dispersion-corrected model). Check it first against the C16 and C18 melting points and the eicosane crystal density, then run a solid–liquid coexistence or interface-pinning simulation on a larger GPU allocation.\n- **Measured value:** the experimental melting point of n-eicosane is 310.0 ± 1.0 K (NIST WebBook average, from `/data/calibration/tier1.json`). That is a measurement, not a MACE-MPA-0 result."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3527,"cache_read_input_tokens":25544,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3527,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"54f27437-f731-4ad1-b63e-9fc22196bbb8","uuid":"46ee8d86-7fee-425b-9ec8-d4a2213256f4","timestamp":"2026-11-01T10:12:44.826Z","request_id":"req_011Cgc2fnqwDi3XN8bni4BmZ"}
